"Sammiel" <lowrysam@gmail.com> writes:
> As the subject asks, what information in the spoilers can or can't,
> even if only in principle, be discovered in-game?
> 
> Clearly some information is trivial: various uses of items, skills and
> intrinsics gained and lost due to various events, etc....
> 
> But some not so: what about probabilities? I would say that these in
> fact can be discovered. Given a large enough number of carefully
> recorded games, you could find out that gems do indeed comprise 8% of
> all randomly generated objects in the main dungeon. Your answer might
> only ever be approximate, but over time it would tend to the correct
> figure.
> 
> [...]
> 
> I think a perfect set of spoilers would be the grand unified theory
> of Nethack. The source is, almost by definition, a perfect set of
> spoilers, but that doesn't mean it's the only one. [...] I claim you
> could even do this just by playing the game - without any access to
> the source code!

I think these two paragraphs are contradictory - or rather, it would
take an infinite amount of time to be certain that your model was the
correct one. It's the problem of induction; if you've played 10000
games in which a polymorph trap hasn't been generated above DL8, how
do you _know_ that it's impossible for them to be so, rather than
there being an extremely small chance that just hasn't happened to
come up yet?

